{"id":"PO-1","kind":"missing","severity":"blocking","requirement":"none","title":"No requirement for the order screen where lines are shown and edited","why":"The brief promises \"add, edit, and remove order lines\" and \"calculate order totals\". R-11 only says a line \"is saved on its order\". R-15 and R-16 define how totals are calculated, but only R-14 says a total is visible. R-04 is the only view requirement, and it covers customers. No requirement says the user can open an order and see its number, customer, date, its lines with their line totals, and the order total. That screen is where the add, edit and remove line flows start, so a build that follows the requirements can leave it out.","suggested":"### R-26 [MUST] View an order\n- Opening an order from the order list or from the customer view shows its number, its customer's name, its date, and its lines in the order they were added (A-06).\n- Each line shows description, quantity, unit price and line total. The order total is shown below the lines.\n- From this screen the user can add a line (R-11), edit a line (R-13), remove a line (R-14), change the date (R-08) and delete the order (R-09).\n- A \"Back\" link returns to the list the user came from (ADR-004).","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-2","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-10","title":"Order list content and how an order is opened are not stated","why":"R-10 says \"The order list can be filtered by customer and by text\" and is sorted \"newest date first\", but never says what each row shows. R-04 lists \"each order's total\" in the customer view but not the order number or date. It also does not say whether a row opens the order. A user who cannot tell orders apart in a list cannot pick the one to work on.","suggested":"- Each row in the order list shows the order number, date, customer name and order total. Clicking a row opens the order (R-26).\n- In the customer view (R-04), each order shows its number, date and total, and clicking it opens the order.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-3","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-11","title":"Total updating after adding or editing a line is only required for removal","why":"R-14 says \"the order total updates at once\", but R-11 (add) and R-13 (edit) say nothing about the total the user sees. A screen that refreshes the total only on removal would meet the text.","suggested":"R-11 and R-13, add: - After saving, the line total and the order total shown on the order update at once, without a reload.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-4","kind":"risk","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-18","title":"Unreadable stored data leaves the user stuck with no way out","why":"R-18 says the app \"shows an error and does not write over it, so the user can recover it\". Export is R-25 [LATER], so there is no way to recover the data from inside the app, and nothing lets the user start again. The user is stuck on the error until they clear localStorage by hand in the browser tools.","suggested":"- The error shows the stored text in a read-only box the user can select and copy.\n- A \"Start with empty data\" button asks for confirmation, warns that the stored data will be replaced, and only then writes an empty data set.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-5","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-19","title":"What the user sees after a failed save is not observable","why":"\"the change is not shown as saved\" can mean either of two things: the form closes and the list stays unchanged, or the form stays open. If the form closes, the user loses what they typed. AGENTS.md rule 6 says \"never lose user data silently\".","suggested":"- If saving fails, an error says the change was not saved. The form stays open with the values the user entered, and the lists still show the data as it was before.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-6","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-05","title":"Confirmation is asked before the app checks for orders","why":"R-05 says \"Deleting asks for confirmation first\" and then \"A customer that still has orders cannot be deleted\". As written, the user confirms a delete and is then told it cannot happen.","suggested":"- If the customer has orders, the app says right away how many orders they have and that those must be deleted first. No confirmation is asked and nothing is removed.\n- If the customer has no orders, the app asks for confirmation and deletes only after the user confirms.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-7","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-08","title":"No validation for the order date","why":"R-07 gives a default date and R-08 says \"The order date can be changed\". No criterion covers a cleared or invalid date, even though the brief asks for \"basic validation\". An order without a date cannot be sorted in R-10's \"newest date first\" list.","suggested":"- An empty or invalid date is refused with a message next to the field; nothing is saved.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-8","kind":"risk","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-07","title":"\"Default today\" may show yesterday's date","why":"R-07 says \"order date (default today)\" and A-05 says it is a calendar date. If today is taken from UTC, a user east of Greenwich who creates an order shortly after midnight sees yesterday's date. Settled by stating which clock is used.","suggested":"- The default date is today's date in the user's local time zone.","proven":false},
{"id":"PO-9","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-10","title":"Order number matching and the sort of filtered results can be read two ways","why":"R-10 says the text \"matches the order number\". It could be an exact match, or contains, so that \"1\" also finds 10 and 11. \"newest date first\" is stated only for \"An empty filter\", and orders that share a date have no defined order.","suggested":"- The text matches when the order number written as digits contains it, or the customer name contains it, case-insensitive, ignoring leading and trailing spaces.\n- Filtered and unfiltered lists are sorted by date, newest first, then by order number, highest first.","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-10","kind":"risk","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-07","title":"Customers with the same name cannot be told apart when picking one","why":"R-01 only needs a non-empty name, so two customers can share a name. R-07 (pick the customer for an order) and R-10 (\"filtered by customer\") give the user a choice by customer, but nothing says how to tell two customers with the same name apart.","suggested":"- Wherever a customer is picked from a list, the name is shown together with the email or phone when they are set.","proven":false},
{"id":"PO-11","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"none","title":"No way to cancel a create or edit form, and nothing said about unsaved input","why":"The brief asks for \"create, edit\" of customers and lines. ADR-004 gives a \"Back\" link on detail screens. No requirement says a form can be cancelled, or what happens to typed input when the user switches tab or presses Back. Settled by the human choosing between discarding the input or asking first.","suggested":"- Every create or edit form has a Cancel button that closes it without saving and leaves the data unchanged.\n- Leaving a form with changed fields (tab, Back) asks for confirmation before the changes are discarded.","proven":false},
{"id":"PO-12","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-12","title":"The comma rule from ADR-003 is missing from the price criterion","why":"R-12 says the unit price is \"entered in euros with at most two decimals\". ADR-003 decided that \"A comma anywhere in the price is refused with the message 'Use a dot for decimals, e.g. 12.50'\". The message the user sees is not part of the acceptance criteria, so it is not checked against R-12.","suggested":"- A price containing a comma is refused with the message \"Use a dot for decimals, e.g. 12.50\"; nothing is saved (ADR-003).","proven":true},
{"id":"PO-13","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-17","title":"Persistence criterion uses implementation words","why":"\"Every change is written to localStorage at once\" describes the code, not something the user sees. The observable part is the second half, \"after a reload all customers, orders and lines are as they were\".","suggested":"- After any change is saved and the page is reloaded or the browser reopened, all customers, orders and lines are as they were.","proven":true}
